    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1973-p13.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The fraction is positive, and its square is \\[ \\begin{aligned} &\\frac{4(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6)^2} {9(2+\\sqrt3)}\\\\ &\\quad=\\frac{4(8+4\\sqrt3)} {9(2+\\sqrt3)}\\\\ &\\quad=\\frac{16(2+\\sqrt3)} {9(2+\\sqrt3)}\\\\ &\\quad=\\frac{16}{9}. \\end{aligned} \\] Therefore the original fraction is \\(\\frac{4}{3}.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "All quantities are positive, so compare the square of the fraction with the squares of the choices",
        "Use \\((\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6)^2=8+4\\sqrt3\\)"
      ],
